The box is a portable, battery-powered recorder for GNSS, IMU and camera data on one GPS-disciplined
timebase. Later it will also run fusion onboard. It is a personal project for hands-on sensor-fusion
experience, which is why each milestone has to end in real numbers. Nothing has been built yet, so
this page describes the intent. Anything it says about the hardware is a claim until the
[open questions](../../reference/open-questions/) say otherwise.

## The hardware

| Item                                                                       | Role                                                         |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| ArduSimple simpleRTK2B Fusion (u-blox ZED-F9R)                             | RTK GNSS and internal IMU; the master clock                  |
| Existing SD logger board                                                   | Independent raw-UBX backup, for PPK ground truth with RTKLIB |
| Raspberry Pi 5 4 GB with Active Cooler                                     | Main computer                                                |
| Innomaker CAM-MIPI296RAW (Sony IMX296 mono global shutter, 1456×1088, M12) | Camera                                                       |
| Raspberry Pi Pico                                                          | Trigger generator, locked to the PPS                         |
| Digilent Analog Discovery 3                                                | Scope and logic analyzer for scripted timing measurements    |
| USB-C panel-mount extension and PD power banks                             | One power input; expect 5 V at 3 A only                      |

## The architecture

<ModelGraph spec={box} />

**Time.** GPS time is the master clock. The F9R's TIMEPULSE at 1 PPS goes to a Pi GPIO, where chrony
disciplines the system clock, and to the Pico, which generates camera triggers at known GPS times.
Chrony takes coarse time from `ubx_mux`, the one process that owns the receiver's USB port, so time
sync works without ROS.

**The stamping rule.** `header.stamp` is the measurement time on the GPS timebase, set at the source.
Arrival time at the Pi is recorded separately, for latency diagnostics only. The stamp counts UNIX
nanoseconds: the receiver's GPS time minus the leap-second count it reports, converted in one helper.
The count is used only once `UBX-NAV-TIMEGPS` marks it valid and `UBX-NAV-TIMELS` says it came from a
constellation rather than the firmware's default, so the box records nothing before then. Each
message also keeps the raw GPS week and time of week, from which continuous GPS time can be recovered
across a leap second.

> [!DECISION] Why UNIX nanoseconds
> Stamps then compare directly with the system clock and agree with every ROS tool, and the rare leap
> second is recoverable from the raw fields. See the [foundations writeup](../../archive/foundations/writeup.md).

**Software.** The Pi runs 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie. ROS 2 Jazzy comes from RoboStack, installed
with Pixi and pinned by a committed `pixi.lock`; chrony and `ubx_mux` run on the host, outside it.
The ROS side is a thin UBX driver of our own, so that we control the stamps, `camera_ros` for frames,
`trigger_bridge` to match frames to their triggers, and MCAP bags split every few minutes. The camera
runs only through the libcamera in the Pixi environment; the one Raspberry Pi OS ships is kept as a
diagnostic. Calibration runs offline on a development machine, in Kalibr, in a ROS 1 container.
Onboard fusion comes later: a GTSAM GNSS and IMU factor graph, then mono VIO.

> [!DECISION] Why Raspberry Pi OS, and ROS 2 from Pixi
> The camera's trigger mode and the PPS overlay both depend on the Pi's kernel, which Raspberry Pi OS
> carries and the camera's vendor documents. Pixi then supplies ROS 2 without a container between the
> nodes and the hardware. See the [foundations writeup](../../archive/foundations/writeup.md).

**Repository.** The repository is laid out flat: `firmware/`, `ros2_ws/`, `host/`, `system/`,
`tools/`, `calib/`, `cad/` and `docker/` at the top level, beside the site and the Pixi manifest. The
directories arrive with M0.

**Power.** One USB-C PD input to the Pi, which powers the F9R and the Pico over USB and the camera
over CSI.

**Mechanical.** One rigid plate carries the F9R board and the camera, with nothing compliant between
the IMU and the camera. The Pi and power hardware sit in a shell around it.
